WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.920 --> 00:00:04.760
I'm Jenna Fisher.
我是珍娜·费舍尔。

2
00:00:04.940 --> 00:00:06.060
And I'm Angela Kinsey.
我是安吉拉·金赛。

3
00:00:06.200 --> 00:00:07.340
We were on The Office together.
我们曾一起出演《办公室》。

4
00:00:07.580 --> 00:00:08.580
And we're best friends.
我们还是最好的朋友。

5
00:00:08.720 --> 00:00:12.420
And now we're doing the Ultimate Office rewatch podcast just for you.
现在，我们专为你制作了这档《办公室》终极重温播客。

6
00:00:12.600 --> 00:00:19.400
Each week, we will break down an episode of The Office and give exclusive behind-the-scenes stories that only two people who were there can tell you.
每周，我们都会剖析一集《办公室》，并分享只有身临其境的我们两人才能告诉你的独家幕后故事。

7
00:00:19.560 --> 00:00:20.560
We're The Office Ladies.
我们是“办公室女士”。

8
00:00:24.740 --> 00:00:25.180
Hello.
大家好。

9
00:00:25.220 --> 00:00:27.120
Are you in your comfy pants?
你穿上舒适的裤子了吗？

10
00:00:27.680 --> 00:00:30.280
Do you have on a t-shirt and maybe some flip-flops?
你是不是穿着T恤，可能还穿着人字拖？

11
00:00:30.720 --> 00:00:32.100
Why are you saying these things?
你为什么这么说？

12
00:00:32.320 --> 00:00:33.280
You can see me.
你能看见我。

13
00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:35.960
I'm talking to you guys out there.
我是在跟外面的听众说话。

14
00:00:35.960 --> 00:00:38.460
Oh, because it's Casual Friday.
哦，因为今天是休闲星期五。

15
00:00:38.460 --> 00:00:40.120
Because it's Casual Friday.
因为今天是休闲星期五。

16
00:00:40.580 --> 00:00:41.340
Oh, yeah.
哦，没错。

17
00:00:41.740 --> 00:00:43.640
Why didn't I dress more casually today?
我今天为什么没穿得更休闲一点？

18
00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:44.660
I don't know.
我不知道。

19
00:00:44.720 --> 00:00:45.800
You look real spiffy.
你看起来真的很时髦。

20
00:00:46.120 --> 00:00:47.200
Well, I know why.
嗯，我知道为什么。

21
00:00:47.240 --> 00:00:48.760
Because we have a special gueSt.
因为我们有一位特别嘉宾。

22
00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:49.480
We do.
没错。

23
00:00:49.860 --> 00:00:54.860
Let's start by saying that Casual Friday is Season 5, Episode 26.
我们先来说说，《休闲星期五》是第五季第26集。

24
00:00:55.580 --> 00:00:56.040
Dang.
天哪。

25
00:00:56.560 --> 00:01:00.100
It was written by Anthony Farrell and directed by Brent Forrester.
这一集由安东尼·法瑞尔编剧，布伦特·福雷斯特执导。

26
00:01:00.400 --> 00:01:01.220
Little tidbit.
一个小花絮。

27
00:01:01.440 --> 00:01:02.140
What is it?
是什么？

28
00:01:02.440 --> 00:01:04.060
Editor was Claire Scanlon.
剪辑师是克莱尔·斯坎伦。

29
00:01:04.060 --> 00:01:06.220
And our DP was Matt Sohn.
我们的摄影指导是马特·索恩。

30
00:01:06.620 --> 00:01:08.320
Hey, I like this team.
嘿，我喜欢这个团队。

31
00:01:08.500 --> 00:01:09.060
I do, too.
我也喜欢。

32
00:01:09.060 --> 00:01:12.220
And it was the first time this team had worked together on an episode.
这是这个团队第一次合作拍摄一集节目。

33
00:01:12.740 --> 00:01:13.260
Wow.
哇。

34
00:01:13.460 --> 00:01:13.740
Yeah.
是啊。

35
00:01:14.040 --> 00:01:14.980
They did a good job.
他们做得很好。

36
00:01:15.080 --> 00:01:15.360
They did.
确实。

37
00:01:15.580 --> 00:01:16.620
Here's your summary.
这是剧情梗概。

38
00:01:16.900 --> 00:01:19.900
It is Casual Friday at Dunder Mifflin.
邓德·米福林公司迎来了休闲星期五。

39
00:01:20.120 --> 00:01:20.640
Finally.
终于。

40
00:01:20.960 --> 00:01:22.060
It has been reinstated.
它又恢复了。

41
00:01:22.720 --> 00:01:23.960
Michael is back.
迈克尔回来了。

42
00:01:24.120 --> 00:01:25.040
He's the manager.
他是经理。

43
00:01:25.060 --> 00:01:26.840
It's Casual Friday again.
又是休闲星期五了。

44
00:01:26.840 --> 00:01:33.100
But some of the employees are taking a few too many liberties with their outfit choices.
但有些员工在服装选择上有点太随意了。

45
00:01:33.660 --> 00:01:35.260
Like, Angela's not happy.
比如，安吉拉很不高兴。

46
00:01:35.420 --> 00:01:37.440
Angela doesn't need to see a grown man's feet.
安吉拉可不想看到成年男人的脚。

47
00:01:37.540 --> 00:01:38.440
Probably ever.
可能永远都不想。

48
00:01:38.740 --> 00:01:42.160
I'm surprised that we didn't see bare feet on Kevin as well.
我很惊讶我们竟然没看到凯文也光着脚。

49
00:01:42.460 --> 00:01:43.140
Well, yeah.
嗯，确实。

50
00:01:43.140 --> 00:01:44.420
But here's the thing.
但重点是。

51
00:01:44.620 --> 00:01:58.000
The sales team is resentful that Pam and Ryan came on as new salespeople and that they get to keep the clients that they stole from everybody else while they were working at Michael Scott Paper Company.
销售团队对帕姆和瑞安以新销售员身份加入感到不满，而且他们还能保留在迈克尔·斯科特造纸公司工作时从其他人那里抢走的客户。

52
00:01:58.000 --> 00:01:59.620
I mean, that's just awkward.
我是说，这太尴尬了。

53
00:01:59.920 --> 00:02:00.220
Yeah.
没错。

54
00:02:00.360 --> 00:02:05.100
So, there's a bit of a rivalry that's going to happen on this episode today.
所以，今天这一集里会发生一些竞争。

55
00:02:05.200 --> 00:02:05.540
Mm-hmm.
嗯哼。

56
00:02:05.900 --> 00:02:09.740
But first, there's a pretty big thing that kicks off this episode.
但首先，这一集开场有一件大事。

57
00:02:09.960 --> 00:02:11.720
This is an epic cold open.
这是一个史诗级的开场戏。

58
00:02:12.180 --> 00:02:15.060
It's the chili spill cold open.
就是凯文洒辣椒的那段开场。

59
00:02:15.300 --> 00:02:16.320
Oh, my gosh.
天哪。

60
00:02:16.420 --> 00:02:18.980
Kevin's recipe, the pride he takes in it.
凯文的食谱，他以此为傲。

61
00:02:19.140 --> 00:02:20.880
He tries to make it up the stairs.
他试图端着它上楼。

62
00:02:20.880 --> 00:02:21.440
He does.
他确实这么做了。

63
00:02:21.560 --> 00:02:22.200
And what happens?
然后发生了什么？

64
00:02:22.600 --> 00:02:23.220
Oh, boy.
噢，天哪。

65
00:02:23.400 --> 00:02:23.860
Oh, boy.
噢，天哪。

66
00:02:23.860 --> 00:02:31.100
Well, we felt like this was the perfect opportunity to speak to the man behind the chili, Brian Baumgartner.
我们觉得这是一个绝佳的机会，可以和辣椒背后的男人——布莱恩·鲍姆加特纳聊聊。

67
00:02:31.380 --> 00:02:32.000
That's right.
没错。

68
00:02:32.140 --> 00:02:34.940
Brian is our Fast Fact 1, 2, and 3 today.
布莱恩是我们今天第一、二、三个快速事实的主角。

69
00:02:35.240 --> 00:02:35.480
Yes.
是的。

70
00:02:35.480 --> 00:02:37.100
We can't wait to talk to him.
我们迫不及待想和他聊聊了。

71
00:02:37.300 --> 00:02:37.900
All right.
好的。

72
00:02:38.040 --> 00:02:38.780
Here he is.
他来了。

73
00:02:44.260 --> 00:02:45.680
Baumgartner, hello.
鲍姆加特纳，你好。

74
00:02:46.740 --> 00:02:49.040
What is happening?
最近怎么样？

75
00:02:49.320 --> 00:02:51.320
I'm so happy to be with you guys.
我很高兴能和你们在一起。

76
00:02:51.660 --> 00:02:52.100
Look at you, buddy.
看看你，伙计。

77
00:02:52.100 --> 00:02:53.140
You have a plant.
你还有盆栽。

78
00:02:53.200 --> 00:02:54.840
I love that you have a plant.
我喜欢你有盆栽这一点。

79
00:02:55.960 --> 00:02:59.680
I was told that brings humanity and warmth.
有人告诉我这能带来人文气息和温暖感。

80
00:02:59.800 --> 00:03:02.760
Oh, what is the award that's over your shoulder?
噢，你肩膀上方那个奖是什么？

81
00:03:03.060 --> 00:03:03.820
You know what?
你知道吗？

82
00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:09.460
No one has ever asked that in all of the Zooms I've been doing for a year and a half.
在我这一年半参加的所有Zoom会议里，从来没有人问过这个问题。

83
00:03:09.880 --> 00:03:11.520
Truly, it always made me laugh.
说真的，它总是让我发笑。

84
00:03:11.720 --> 00:03:15.220
I don't have my college diploma, but I'll read.
我没有大学毕业证书，但我会读。

85
00:03:15.220 --> 00:03:23.560
I have Penn Foster College, a certificate of completion, which I always thought was a good
我有宾福斯特学院的结业证书，我一直觉得这对会计部的凯文·马龙来说是个不错的

86
00:03:23.560 --> 00:03:26.240
joke, for Kevin Malone in accounting.
笑话。

87
00:03:26.420 --> 00:03:28.040
That is awesome.
那太棒了。

88
00:03:28.640 --> 00:03:30.080
A certificate of completion.
一张结业证书。

89
00:03:30.360 --> 00:03:35.960
Maybe all diplomas say that, but I thought, oh, they're doing a little funny joke.
也许所有的毕业证书都这么写，但我当时想，噢，他们是在开个小玩笑。

90
00:03:36.160 --> 00:03:38.360
Like, it's not really a degree in accounting.
就好像，那并不是真正的会计学位。

91
00:03:38.560 --> 00:03:40.820
He took a weekend course.
他只是参加了一个周末课程。

92
00:03:40.820 --> 00:03:42.520
He has a piece of paper for participating.
他有一张参与证明。

93
00:03:43.020 --> 00:03:48.120
Well, listen, we are here to talk about The Office with you and obviously about this amazing
好吧，听着，我们今天来是为了和你聊聊《办公室》，当然还有这个精彩的

94
00:03:48.120 --> 00:03:48.620
cold open.
开场戏。

95
00:03:48.760 --> 00:03:53.560
But Brian, we always ask our guests, how did you get your job on The Office?
但是布莱恩，我们总是问嘉宾，你是怎么得到《办公室》这份工作的？

96
00:03:53.900 --> 00:03:55.980
I was walking down Hollywood Boulevard.
我当时正走在好莱坞大道上。

97
00:03:56.140 --> 00:03:56.440
No.
不。

98
00:03:56.760 --> 00:03:58.120
Oh, I like the start of that.
噢，我喜欢这个开头。

99
00:03:58.240 --> 00:03:58.660
No.
不。

100
00:03:58.860 --> 00:04:03.500
I feel like my story always seems like a humble brag or something.
我觉得我的故事听起来总像是在凡尔赛或者什么的。

101
00:04:03.500 --> 00:04:13.040
But my truth is, I moved to Los Angeles and I considered it my job to just watch television.
但我的实情是，我搬到了洛杉矶，我把看电视当成了我的工作。

102
00:04:13.420 --> 00:04:17.900
And so I started watching television shows because I was like, well, if I get an audition
所以我开始看电视节目，因为我想，好吧，如果我去参加这个节目的试镜，

103
00:04:17.900 --> 00:04:20.600
on this show, don't I need to know what's the style?
难道我不该了解一下它的风格吗？

104
00:04:20.840 --> 00:04:22.120
What's the form?
它的形式是什么？

105
00:04:22.300 --> 00:04:24.440
How do the characters generally behave?
角色通常是怎么表现的？

106
00:04:24.660 --> 00:04:30.680
So I started watching television and I watched this show called The Office, the British version
所以我开始看电视，我看了那个叫《办公室》的剧，是英版的《办公室》，

107
00:04:30.680 --> 00:04:36.240
of The Office, and I started working with a manager and I called him and I said, if there
我当时开始和一个经纪人合作，我打电话给他说，如果有一部剧要上电视，

108
00:04:36.240 --> 00:04:43.080
is a show that is that is going to be on television, because they announced they were doing a pilot,
因为他们宣布要拍试播集了，

109
00:04:43.240 --> 00:04:44.460
I was like, this is the show for me.
我就觉得，这就是适合我的剧。

110
00:04:46.480 --> 00:04:52.440
And I had an agent at the time and the agent who I called and said the same thing to said,
当时我有个代理人，我打电话给他说了一样的话，他说，

111
00:04:52.440 --> 00:05:02.320
well, they're looking for unknown people, but not like you unknown, not like totally unknown.
嗯，他们想找默默无闻的人，但不是你这种默默无闻，不是那种完全没名气的。

112
00:05:02.640 --> 00:05:04.240
I was like, all right, thank you very much.
我就说，好吧，非常感谢。

113
00:05:04.400 --> 00:05:04.700
Thanks a lot.
多谢了。

114
00:05:05.440 --> 00:05:10.780
But my manager called and called and called Allison Jones and I went in and I met with Phyllis
但我经纪人不停地给艾莉森·琼斯打电话，后来我去了，见了菲利斯，

115
00:05:10.780 --> 00:05:18.380
and I read and then she passed me on to Allison Jones and I read with her and I got an appointment
我试读了剧本，然后她把我引荐给艾莉森·琼斯，我和她一起读了剧本，我得到了一个试镜机会，

116
00:05:18.380 --> 00:05:24.760
for the role of Stanley and I knew that was not the part for me.
是斯坦利这个角色，但我知道那不是适合我的角色。

117
00:05:24.920 --> 00:05:29.920
I knew that the part for me was in the British version, his name was Keith.
我知道适合我的角色是英版里的那个，他叫基思。

118
00:05:30.260 --> 00:05:33.240
And I was like that, I get that comedy.
我当时就觉得，那种喜剧我懂。

119
00:05:33.580 --> 00:05:42.900
So I went in to this big call and I read the role of Stanley as though I were Kevin.
所以我去参加了那场大型试镜，我用凯文的方式试读了斯坦利的角色。

120
00:05:43.100 --> 00:05:43.940
Oh, wow.
噢，哇。

121
00:05:44.480 --> 00:05:45.280
So smart, Brian.
太聪明了，布莱恩。

122
00:05:46.520 --> 00:05:48.240
Well, or including the disaster.
嗯，或者说也可能是一场灾难。

123
00:05:48.380 --> 00:05:50.500
They could have been like, what is this?
他们当时完全可以觉得，这到底是什么鬼？

124
00:05:50.500 --> 00:05:51.240
Smart now.
现在看来很聪明。

125
00:05:52.440 --> 00:05:53.560
But right.
但确实。

126
00:05:53.880 --> 00:06:01.640
So I went, I was there at the same time Oscar Nunez was there and Leslie David Baker were there.
所以我去了，当时奥斯卡·努内兹和莱斯利·大卫·贝克也在那儿。

127
00:06:02.200 --> 00:06:05.220
I can't remember who all else, but I know that they were there.
我不记得还有谁了，但我知道他们当时都在。

128
00:06:05.540 --> 00:06:08.920
And so I did it.
然后我就演了。

129
00:06:09.040 --> 00:06:10.540
They said, thank you.
他们说，谢谢。

130
00:06:10.660 --> 00:06:14.520
And I left and thought, well, that was a failed experiment.
我离开时心想，好吧，那是一次失败的实验。

131
00:06:14.800 --> 00:06:16.780
And Allison Jones ran down the hall.
结果艾莉森·琼斯跑过走廊追了过来。

132
00:06:16.780 --> 00:06:20.760
She said, hey, you know, they actually want to look at this other character.
她说，嘿，你知道吗，他们其实想看看那个别的角色。

133
00:06:21.000 --> 00:06:24.140
And I, in my head, I went, big go.
我当时心里想，大功告成。

134
00:06:25.300 --> 00:06:27.120
And went in, read that.
然后进去，试读了那个角色。

135
00:06:27.340 --> 00:06:29.640
And, and that's, that's how it happened.
然后，事情就是这样发生的。

136
00:06:29.900 --> 00:06:34.720
But you never said, Allison, I think I'd be a better fit for Kevin.
但你从来没对艾莉森说，艾莉森，我觉得我更适合演凯文。

137
00:06:35.060 --> 00:06:36.680
Would you give me a shot at it?
你能给我个机会试试吗？

138
00:06:36.680 --> 00:06:40.720
You just took this risk of reading Stanley like Kevin.
你只是冒着风险用凯文的方式去试读了斯坦利。

139
00:06:41.940 --> 00:06:42.380
Yes.
是的。

140
00:06:42.800 --> 00:06:46.100
Because, well, because again, I was totally unknown, right?
因为，嗯，还是那句话，我当时完全是个无名小卒，对吧？

141
00:06:46.220 --> 00:06:49.980
Like I didn't have, I didn't have the experience.
我没有，我没有那种经验。

142
00:06:50.160 --> 00:06:51.900
I mean, now, of course, that's what I would do.
我是说，现在的话，我当然会那么做。

143
00:06:52.000 --> 00:06:53.780
But yeah, no, I didn't.
但当时，没有，我没那么做。

144
00:06:53.880 --> 00:06:54.560
I didn't.
我没做。

145
00:06:54.560 --> 00:06:56.600
I was, you know, happy to have an opportunity.
我当时，你知道，能有机会就很高兴了。

146
00:06:56.800 --> 00:06:59.920
You know, I had just moved to Los Angeles a few months before.
你知道，我才刚搬到洛杉矶几个月。

147
00:07:00.460 --> 00:07:00.820
Wow.
哇。

148
00:07:01.320 --> 00:07:01.520
Of that.
在那之前。

149
00:07:01.700 --> 00:07:02.980
Yeah, I knew that.
是啊，我知道。

150
00:07:02.980 --> 00:07:03.500
I knew that.
我知道。

151
00:07:03.540 --> 00:07:08.860
Because the first day on set when we got to know each other, just briefly chatting, Oscar
因为在片场的第一天，我们互相认识的时候，只是简单聊了聊，奥斯卡和我

152
00:07:08.860 --> 00:07:12.480
and I were sort of commiserating about how many years we'd been out here plugging away.
当时还在感叹我们在这里摸爬滚打了多少年。

153
00:07:12.660 --> 00:07:14.020
And we're like, what about you, Brian?
然后我们问，布莱恩，你呢？

154
00:07:14.180 --> 00:07:17.300
And he was like, I've lived here like eight weeks.
他说，我才搬来这里大概八周。

155
00:07:17.420 --> 00:07:18.060
We're like, what?
我们说，什么？

156
00:07:19.000 --> 00:07:20.280
I've been here two months.
我才来了两个月。

157
00:07:20.420 --> 00:07:21.820
We're like, holy crap.
我们说，天哪。

158
00:07:21.820 --> 00:07:27.340
Well, the thing that's like standing out to me are the number of people who came in for
嗯，让我印象深刻的是，有很多人来试镜的角色

159
00:07:27.340 --> 00:07:29.840
a role other than the one they wound up in.
并不是他们最终得到的那个角色。

160
00:07:30.380 --> 00:07:32.160
Angela, you first came in for Pam.
安吉拉，你最初是来试镜帕姆的。

161
00:07:32.400 --> 00:07:36.220
But then also, Ed originally auditioned for Michael Scott.
但还有，艾德最初试镜的是迈克尔·斯科特。

162
00:07:36.560 --> 00:07:38.660
John was brought in to play Dwight.
约翰最初是被叫来演德怀特的。

163
00:07:38.980 --> 00:07:39.460
Yeah.
没错。

164
00:07:39.760 --> 00:07:42.220
And Rain auditioned for Michael Scott as well.
雷恩也试镜了迈克尔·斯科特。

165
00:07:42.520 --> 00:07:43.260
I know.
我知道。

166
00:07:43.420 --> 00:07:44.180
That's crazy.
太疯狂了。

167
00:07:44.440 --> 00:07:45.520
A lot of people.
很多人都是这样。

168
00:07:45.600 --> 00:07:45.780
Yeah.
是啊。

169
00:07:45.780 --> 00:07:52.800
I feel like on the set, you and John Krasinski were pretty good buds.
我觉得在片场，你和约翰·卡拉辛斯基关系挺好的。

170
00:07:53.160 --> 00:07:54.660
Yeah, you guys became good pals.
没错，你们成了好哥们。

171
00:07:54.820 --> 00:07:56.560
And the four of us, I would say.
我觉得我们四个也是。

172
00:07:56.720 --> 00:08:01.640
You and John, me and Angela, we could often be found in John Krasinski's trailer during
你和约翰，我和安吉拉，午餐时间经常能在约翰·卡拉辛斯基的拖车里

173
00:08:01.640 --> 00:08:02.280
lunchtime.
找到我们。

174
00:08:02.280 --> 00:08:06.120
You guys had some heated Madden football matches.
你们当时玩《麦登橄榄球》玩得很激烈。

175
00:08:06.120 --> 00:08:07.540
I mean, for years.
我是说，玩了好几年。

176
00:08:07.540 --> 00:08:15.760
Now, I hear people talking about, you know, what a family we were and the crew and the
现在，我听到人们谈论我们当时像一家人一样，剧组和演员们

177
00:08:15.760 --> 00:08:16.220
caSt.
有多亲密。

178
00:08:16.400 --> 00:08:18.500
And all of that, by the way, is true.
顺便说一句，这些都是真的。

179
00:08:18.900 --> 00:08:22.180
But then, undoubtedly, someone will mention like lunch.
但毫无疑问，总有人会提到午餐。

180
00:08:22.180 --> 00:08:24.220
And at lunch, you know, you would sit with this person.
午餐的时候，你知道，你会和某个人坐在一起。

181
00:08:24.600 --> 00:08:26.060
I didn't sit with anyone.
我没和任何人坐在一起。

182
00:08:26.540 --> 00:08:35.040
John Krasinski and I played Madden football in his trailer essentially every single time.
约翰·卡拉辛斯基和我基本上每次午餐都在他的拖车里玩《麦登橄榄球》。

183
00:08:35.040 --> 00:08:35.500
That's right.
没错。

184
00:08:35.860 --> 00:08:37.020
It was weird.
那很奇怪。

185
00:08:37.020 --> 00:08:38.760
We could hear you guys.
我们能听到你们的声音。

186
00:08:39.200 --> 00:08:43.820
If we walked past the trailer at lunch, you could literally hear like, oh, come on.
如果午餐时路过拖车，真的能听到你们喊，噢，拜托。

187
00:08:44.660 --> 00:08:46.700
Sometimes you would let Rain play.
有时候你会让雷恩玩。

188
00:08:47.020 --> 00:08:47.480
Mm-hmm.
嗯。

189
00:08:47.680 --> 00:08:49.720
But it seemed like begrudgingly.
但看起来很不情愿。

190
00:08:49.940 --> 00:08:53.300
You'd be like, oh, I guess we have to let someone else play.
你会说，噢，我想我们得让别人玩一下了。

191
00:08:53.780 --> 00:08:55.080
But it was the two of you.
但基本上就是你们俩。

192
00:08:55.260 --> 00:08:59.560
And I would like to know, are you willing to go on record and say who was better?
我想知道，你愿不愿意公开说一下谁更厉害？

193
00:09:00.060 --> 00:09:02.260
Because I have a memory of who was better.
因为我记得谁更厉害。

194
00:09:02.440 --> 00:09:03.580
Who do you think was better?
你觉得谁更厉害？

195
00:09:03.900 --> 00:09:04.240
Mm-hmm.
嗯。

196
00:09:05.660 --> 00:09:06.040
I.
我。

197
00:09:06.060 --> 00:09:06.460
Uh-oh.
噢哦。

198
00:09:07.240 --> 00:09:10.940
Well, if I say that we were even, that sounds like I'm hedging.
嗯，如果我说我们旗鼓相当，听起来像是在推诿。

199
00:09:11.280 --> 00:09:12.400
But I do.
但我确实是这么认为的。

200
00:09:12.640 --> 00:09:24.080
We kind of, we both had teams that if we were allowed on that day to play those teams, the other person couldn't beat us.
我们都有各自擅长的球队，如果那天允许用那些球队，对方就赢不了我们。

201
00:09:24.080 --> 00:09:28.280
Mine, weird, I wasn't a fan, was the Chargers.
我的那个，怪怪的，我不太喜欢，是闪电队。

202
00:09:28.780 --> 00:09:31.120
And so, this is so great.
所以，这太棒了。

203
00:09:31.440 --> 00:09:32.620
We called it the Gates play.
我们管它叫盖茨战术。

204
00:09:32.800 --> 00:09:38.520
Listen, if you ever play Old Madden and Antonio Gates in the scene, it's a touchdown every time.
听着，如果你玩老版《麦登橄榄球》，只要安东尼奥·盖茨在场上，每次都能达阵。

205
00:09:38.520 --> 00:09:43.600
So, yeah, there's a pro tip for you Old Madden players.
所以，没错，这是给老版《麦登》玩家的一个专业建议。

206
00:09:44.960 --> 00:09:46.820
Yeah, that's what I would say.
没错，我也会这么说。

207
00:09:46.980 --> 00:09:48.760
He would get mad when he loSt.
他输了会很生气。

208
00:09:48.760 --> 00:09:49.780
I will say that.
我得说实话。

209
00:09:50.000 --> 00:09:51.940
He would get way more mad than I would.
他比我生气得多。

210
00:09:51.940 --> 00:09:52.300
Oh, yeah.
哦，没错。

211
00:09:52.420 --> 00:09:53.240
And then grumpy.
然后就会变得闷闷不乐。

212
00:09:53.840 --> 00:09:54.320
Grumpers.
闹脾气。

213
00:09:54.320 --> 00:09:57.400
Because I was going to say, Brian, I think you were the better player.
因为我正想说，布莱恩，我觉得你玩得更好。

214
00:09:57.720 --> 00:10:05.200
My memory from the lunches where I watched you guys play was you winning and then John getting pissed.
我对你们午餐时玩游戏的记忆是，你赢了，然后约翰气得不行。

215
00:10:05.660 --> 00:10:07.400
That's what you held on to.
你记得的倒是这个。

216
00:10:07.600 --> 00:10:13.320
Well, I remember you guys doing the Dunder Mifflin Fantasy Football League, which is still going strong.
嗯，我记得你们搞了个邓德·米夫林梦幻橄榄球联盟，现在还在火热进行中。

217
00:10:13.320 --> 00:10:24.220
I have been told this week's standings are that John is in fourth, Rain is in fifth, and Brian, you are in ninth.
有人告诉我这周的排名是约翰第四，雷恩第五，布莱恩，你是第九。

218
00:10:25.500 --> 00:10:27.020
So, here's the thing.
所以，情况是这样的。

219
00:10:27.300 --> 00:10:30.760
I played Rain this week.
我这周和雷恩对阵。

220
00:10:30.900 --> 00:10:35.060
And by the way, when you all hear this, the stands will be different.
顺便说一下，等你们听到这段时，排名肯定已经变了。

221
00:10:35.360 --> 00:10:36.320
Spoiler alert.
剧透预警。

222
00:10:37.100 --> 00:10:39.980
I lost to Rain this week by a point.
我这周以一分之差输给了雷恩。

223
00:10:40.320 --> 00:10:42.420
And it flipped everything.
这把一切都颠倒了。

224
00:10:42.420 --> 00:10:46.140
So, yeah, I'm in trouble.
所以，没错，我有麻烦了。

225
00:10:46.300 --> 00:10:48.300
But I think I'm four and four.
但我记得我的战绩是四胜四负。

226
00:10:49.200 --> 00:10:51.740
Rain is, I think, five and three.
雷恩好像是五胜三负。

227
00:10:52.180 --> 00:10:54.580
So, like, it literally flipped everything.
所以，真的，一切都反转了。

228
00:10:54.980 --> 00:11:00.600
I was going through our fan mail, Brian, and a frequently asked question was about your voice.
布莱恩，我刚才在看粉丝来信，有个高频问题是关于你的声音的。

229
00:11:00.800 --> 00:11:06.220
Because you very much do not sound like Kevin in real life.
因为你在现实生活中听起来一点也不像凯文。

230
00:11:06.220 --> 00:11:10.980
And people were curious, how did you develop this voice for the character of Kevin?
大家很好奇，你是怎么为凯文这个角色设计出这种声音的？

231
00:11:13.480 --> 00:11:15.720
Well, look, I, okay.
嗯，你看，我，好吧。

232
00:11:15.720 --> 00:11:24.060
So, going back, and my manager and I, who I'm still with now, all of these years later, we still have this joke.
回想起来，我和我的经纪人——我现在依然和他合作，这么多年过去了——我们一直有个笑话。

233
00:11:24.060 --> 00:11:30.100
So, the casting notice about Kevin was the following.
当时关于凯文的选角通知是这样的。

234
00:11:30.220 --> 00:11:32.560
And I know it's true because I've said it so many times.
我知道这是真的，因为我已经说过很多次了。

235
00:11:32.560 --> 00:11:40.640
The only thing remarkable about Kevin is that he is remarkably unremarkable.
凯文唯一引人注目的地方就是他极其平庸。

236
00:11:41.940 --> 00:11:49.660
And so, our joke is always, like, my manager saying to Alison Jones and everyone, you've got to meet this guy.
所以，我们的笑话一直都是，我经纪人会对艾莉森·琼斯他们说，你们一定要见见这家伙。

237
00:11:49.700 --> 00:11:52.080
He is so unremarkable.
他太平庸了。

238
00:11:52.080 --> 00:11:55.100
He is just the most unremarkable.
他简直平庸到了极点。

239
00:11:56.180 --> 00:12:02.740
So, that's really where it started, which, again, really came from the character of Keith in the British version.
所以，这就是一切的起点，再次强调，这确实源于英版里的基思这个角色。

240
00:12:03.240 --> 00:12:07.260
I was, like, a theater actor, right?
我以前是个戏剧演员，对吧？

241
00:12:07.420 --> 00:12:12.960
Like, for me, the construction of character, that's what I feel like I did, right?
对我来说，构建角色，我觉得我就是这么做的，对吧？

242
00:12:12.960 --> 00:12:14.880
So, I wasn't really an improv guy.
所以我并不是那种即兴表演者。

243
00:12:14.880 --> 00:12:17.360
It was, for me, about the character.
对我而言，关键在于角色本身。

244
00:12:17.360 --> 00:12:23.340
And so, for me, it's not like putting on a voice.
所以对我来说，这不像是在刻意模仿某种声音。

245
00:12:24.380 --> 00:12:29.100
But it's really about the body, the way the body moves.
而更多是关于身体，关于身体的动作方式。

246
00:12:29.320 --> 00:12:31.800
I know this is what many people are turning off the show right now.
我知道很多人现在肯定要把节目关掉了。

247
00:12:31.800 --> 00:12:32.640
Oh, Jenna is right now.
哦，珍娜现在就在关。

248
00:12:32.760 --> 00:12:33.500
You should see Jenna.
你应该看看珍娜。

249
00:12:33.500 --> 00:12:34.620
I'm on the edge of my seat.
我正全神贯注地听着呢。

250
00:12:35.120 --> 00:12:37.340
You are speaking to her theater nerd heart.
你这番话正中她那颗戏剧书呆子的心。

251
00:12:38.500 --> 00:12:38.960
Yes.
是的。

252
00:12:38.960 --> 00:12:39.480
I love it.
我很喜欢。

253
00:12:39.780 --> 00:12:40.700
I'm hearing you.
我明白你的意思。

254
00:12:41.140 --> 00:12:43.720
You created your character in your body.
你是在身体里塑造了这个角色。

255
00:12:43.720 --> 00:12:46.980
And that body placement informed your voice.
而这种身体姿态影响了你的声音。

256
00:12:47.200 --> 00:12:49.320
So, it came from the whole body.
所以，它是从整个身体里发出来的。

257
00:12:49.540 --> 00:12:51.220
It didn't start just in your throat.
它不仅仅是从喉咙开始的。

258
00:12:52.440 --> 00:12:52.900
Correct.
没错。

259
00:12:52.900 --> 00:12:58.980
And the way of speaking sort of becomes sort of a manifestation of that, right?
而这种说话方式在某种程度上成了它的体现，对吧？

260
00:12:59.140 --> 00:13:03.440
But for Kevin, my idea was two things.
但对于凯文，我的想法有两点。

261
00:13:03.620 --> 00:13:06.860
One is that he's not aware of his size in space.
第一点是他对自己所占的空间没有意识。

262
00:13:07.180 --> 00:13:09.560
So, hence me, like, bumping into Angela.
所以，这就是我撞到安吉拉的原因。

263
00:13:09.560 --> 00:13:10.340
Yeah, that's why I'm laughing.
是啊，所以我才笑。

264
00:13:10.340 --> 00:13:10.600
Right?
对吧？

265
00:13:10.620 --> 00:13:12.580
There's no, that was one thing.
没错，那是其中一点。

266
00:13:12.700 --> 00:13:17.120
And the second thing was that if you imagine my torso, right?
第二点是，如果你想象一下我的躯干，对吧？

267
00:13:17.240 --> 00:13:19.680
He didn't rotate his hips side to side.
他不会左右转动臀部。

268
00:13:19.680 --> 00:13:23.380
So, if he needed to look in one direction, right?
所以，如果他需要看向某个方向，对吧？

269
00:13:23.440 --> 00:13:29.140
His whole body then would have to turn, like, shuffle step to go in that direction.
他的整个身体就必须转过去，比如，挪着小碎步朝那个方向走。

270
00:13:29.480 --> 00:13:30.900
But let me be clear.
但我得说明白。

271
00:13:31.320 --> 00:13:34.880
There was a progression to the development of the character, too, right?
这个角色的发展也是有一个过程的，对吧？

272
00:13:35.060 --> 00:13:41.940
And so, I get asked sometimes, like, hmm, your voice kind of changed from, you know, season one and through the show.
所以，有时人们会问我，嗯，你的声音从第一季到整部剧好像变了。

273
00:13:41.940 --> 00:13:54.800
And so, my justification was in season one, Kevin was very, very nervous and scared of the cameras.
我的解释是，在第一季里，凯文非常非常紧张，害怕镜头。

274
00:13:55.020 --> 00:14:00.320
And so, when the cameras were around, he retreated into his shell like a turtle.
所以当镜头在周围时，他就像乌龟一样缩进了壳里。

275
00:14:00.320 --> 00:14:08.340
And then, as he became more comfortable or became even friendly with some of the crew, more of his true personality came out.
然后，随着他变得更自在，甚至和一些工作人员变得友好，他更多的真实个性就显露出来了。

276
00:14:08.360 --> 00:14:08.780
Oh.
噢。

277
00:14:09.060 --> 00:14:09.900
I like that.
我喜欢这个说法。

278
00:14:10.100 --> 00:14:10.920
I like that, too.
我也喜欢。

279
00:14:10.940 --> 00:14:12.200
I think that's very good.
我觉得这非常好。

280
00:14:12.660 --> 00:14:17.020
Well, listen, should we talk about this famous Chili Cold Open?
那么，听着，我们要聊聊那个著名的“辣椒开场戏”吗？

281
00:14:17.140 --> 00:14:18.560
I mean, Brian, we have got to.
我是说，布莱恩，我们必须得聊聊。

282
00:14:18.680 --> 00:14:20.440
I brought my Kevin's Chili mug.
我带了我的凯文辣椒马克杯。

283
00:14:20.860 --> 00:14:22.220
I am here for it.
我就是为此而来的。

284
00:14:22.380 --> 00:14:22.430
There you go.
给你。

285
00:14:22.520 --> 00:14:24.820
I brought chili for lunch today.
我今天午饭带了辣椒。

286
00:14:24.960 --> 00:14:25.560
Did you really?
真的吗？

287
00:14:25.560 --> 00:14:28.040
I did, if you can believe it.
真的，如果你敢相信的话。

288
00:14:28.160 --> 00:14:30.140
That's how on brand we are today.
我们今天真是太契合主题了。

289
00:14:30.680 --> 00:14:34.420
So, so many people wrote in about this cold open, Brian.
布莱恩，有好多人写信来问这个开场戏。

290
00:14:34.560 --> 00:14:36.120
It is so beloved, as you know.
如你所知，它非常受欢迎。

291
00:14:36.360 --> 00:14:40.780
But it is also only 36 seconds long.
但它其实只有36秒长。

292
00:14:40.960 --> 00:14:42.000
I clocked it.
我掐表算过。

293
00:14:42.600 --> 00:14:47.720
And here is how it is described on Dunderpedia, which I thought was kind of amazing.
这是Dunderpedia上对它的描述，我觉得非常精彩。

294
00:14:48.660 --> 00:14:55.740
It says the cold open with Kevin's Chili has become one of the iconic moments of the show.
上面写着，凯文做辣椒的开场戏已经成为了该剧的标志性时刻之一。

295
00:14:56.000 --> 00:15:03.540
It takes a low-status character, offers him a high-status moment, and then mercilessly takes it away.
它选取了一个地位低下的角色，给了他一个高光时刻，然后又无情地将其夺走。

296
00:15:04.060 --> 00:15:11.500
People relate to the moment of private embarrassment and the desperation of trying to rescue something that cannot be saved.
人们对那种私下里的尴尬，以及试图挽救无法挽回之物的绝望感同身受。

297
00:15:11.700 --> 00:15:15.340
The scene teeters between comedy and heartbreak.
这一幕在喜剧和心碎之间摇摆。

298
00:15:15.340 --> 00:15:18.100
Like, in 36 seconds.
就这，在36秒之内。

299
00:15:18.740 --> 00:15:19.220
Yeah.
是啊。

300
00:15:19.760 --> 00:15:21.440
You know, people come up to me all the time.
你知道，人们总是过来找我。

301
00:15:21.580 --> 00:15:25.840
That's the moment that more often than not is referred to.
那通常就是大家所提到的那个瞬间。

302
00:15:27.000 --> 00:15:29.880
And there are now, for me, two types of people.
对我来说，现在的人分两种。

303
00:15:30.020 --> 00:15:35.280
There are some people who are like, oh, my God, that is so hilarious.
有些人会觉得，天哪，这太搞笑了。

304
00:15:35.320 --> 00:15:36.300
I love it.
我太喜欢了。

305
00:15:36.420 --> 00:15:39.220
And then there are people who say, like, I can't watch it.
还有些人会说，我看不下去。

306
00:15:39.320 --> 00:15:40.100
It breaks my heart.
这让我心碎。

307
00:15:40.100 --> 00:15:41.540
I feel so bad for Kevin.
我真为凯文感到难过。

308
00:15:41.540 --> 00:15:45.360
Now, I think, in truth, it's somewhere in the middle.
但我觉得，事实应该介于两者之间。

309
00:15:45.700 --> 00:15:47.720
Like, I think that there's both.
我觉得两者兼有。

310
00:15:47.920 --> 00:15:53.240
I think, you know, there's some physical comedy, but I think with a heartbreaking moment.
我觉得既有肢体喜剧的成分，也有令人心碎的时刻。

311
00:15:55.000 --> 00:15:56.800
I never knew.
我从没想过。

312
00:15:57.040 --> 00:16:07.880
I mean, never could have imagined that that one moment that was shot alone with nobody else around would become what it has today.
我是说，我从没想过那个独自拍摄、周围空无一人的瞬间，会演变成今天这个样子。

313
00:16:07.880 --> 00:16:13.420
Well, we got a fan mail flurry of emails.
我们收到了大量的粉丝邮件。

314
00:16:13.760 --> 00:16:15.020
I dare say blizzard.
我甚至敢说是暴风雪般的邮件。

315
00:16:16.620 --> 00:16:19.500
This is, by the way, this is Jenna Terminology.
顺便说一下，这是珍娜术语。

316
00:16:19.760 --> 00:16:20.520
She has a few.
她有几个这样的词。

317
00:16:21.520 --> 00:16:23.200
I've coined some phrases.
我创造了一些短语。

318
00:16:23.400 --> 00:16:24.460
We both have.
我们俩都有。

319
00:16:24.780 --> 00:16:27.860
But the fan mail flurry slash blizzard.
但粉丝邮件阵雨，或者说暴风雪。

320
00:16:28.000 --> 00:16:28.820
Fan mail flurry.
粉丝邮件阵雨。

321
00:16:29.500 --> 00:16:32.280
What can you tell us about shooting this?
关于拍摄这一幕，你能告诉我们什么吗？

322
00:16:32.420 --> 00:16:42.740
Because it is just a voiceover with your action of carrying this big pot of beloved chili up some stairs.
因为那只是配音，配合你端着那一大锅心爱的辣椒走上楼梯的动作。

323
00:16:42.840 --> 00:16:45.100
You're bringing it into the office.
你把它带进办公室。

324
00:16:46.260 --> 00:16:48.800
We're told this is an annual event.
我们被告知这是一年一度的活动。

325
00:16:48.960 --> 00:16:55.200
You've brought in your chili, and then it spills everywhere, and you try desperately to clean it up.
你带来了辣椒，结果洒得到处都是，然后你拼命地想要清理干净。

326
00:16:55.860 --> 00:16:57.060
How did you do it?
你是怎么做到的？

327
00:16:57.060 --> 00:17:00.300
I'm tremendously proud of this now.
我现在对此感到无比自豪。

328
00:17:00.480 --> 00:17:04.240
The actual main portion was one take.
主要的洒出过程是一次拍成的。

329
00:17:04.480 --> 00:17:04.820
The spill?
洒出来的那一下？

330
00:17:04.980 --> 00:17:05.160
The spill.
就是洒出来的那一下。

331
00:17:05.280 --> 00:17:06.280
That's what we heard.
我们听说的就是这个。

332
00:17:06.440 --> 00:17:07.260
One take.
一次拍成。

333
00:17:07.540 --> 00:17:09.100
The spill was one take.
洒出来的那一下是一次拍成的。

334
00:17:09.400 --> 00:17:12.620
So they had done, obviously, some prep, right?
所以他们显然做了一些准备，对吧？

335
00:17:12.700 --> 00:17:13.900
Because this was going to be a mess.
因为这肯定会弄得一团糟。

336
00:17:14.000 --> 00:17:15.600
There was no question this was going to be a mess.
毫无疑问，这肯定会弄得一团糟。

337
00:17:16.220 --> 00:17:18.940
And we'd rehearsed it, how it was going to go.
我们排练过，知道会怎么进行。

338
00:17:19.100 --> 00:17:26.300
Obviously, it was all about the moment of spill having that look and feel as realistic as possible, right?
显然，关键在于让洒出的那一刻看起来尽可能真实，对吧？

339
00:17:26.300 --> 00:17:38.120
And that it happened in the, you know, we didn't have marks on the show, but it happened in generally the correct place for the cameras to be able to pick that up, right?
而且它发生的地点，你知道，我们节目里没有标记，但它发生的位置大致正确，让摄像机能够捕捉到，对吧？

340
00:17:38.720 --> 00:17:44.300
So Phil Shea came to me, like, kind of like a secret, like back channel meeting.
所以菲尔·谢伊来找我，就像那种秘密的、私下的会面。

341
00:17:44.940 --> 00:17:54.240
And he says, all right, so just so you know, we only have three pieces of carpet, so we can't do any more than that.
他说，好吧，你要知道，我们只有三块地毯，所以我们不能拍超过三次。

342
00:17:54.240 --> 00:18:04.340
And the carpet, in case there was an accident, was from the front door around, let's call it Pam's reception desk, all the way to the Dwight and Jim clump.
以防万一发生意外，那地毯是从前门绕过帕姆的前台，一直铺到德怀特和吉姆的办公区。

343
00:18:04.440 --> 00:18:15.740
I mean, that was a huge piece of the carpet that replicated the carpet that existed there, and that they laid down and tacked in and made it appear as though that was the carpet.
那是一大块地毯，复制了那里原有的地毯，他们把它铺好、固定住，让它看起来就像原来的地毯一样。

344
00:18:15.740 --> 00:18:19.340
And they said they had three pieces of it, and that was it, so we needed to go.
他们说只有三块，不能再多了，所以我们必须一次成功。

345
00:18:21.040 --> 00:18:26.700
So what I have said to many people is that that was all planned for.
所以我对很多人说过，那一切都是计划好的。

346
00:18:27.360 --> 00:18:35.180
Cleaning me wasn't really, that wasn't really, no one had any idea what would happen.
至于清理我，那真的没计划，没人知道会发生什么。

347
00:18:35.180 --> 00:18:39.960
Because I was stained, like I was, your hands were like orange.
因为我被染上了颜色，我的手就像橙色的一样。

348
00:18:40.520 --> 00:18:48.260
And I remember, probably my last public shower, but there were like, it felt like 14 people in the shower with me,
我记得，那可能是我最后一次在公共场合洗澡，当时感觉有14个人和我一起在淋浴间里，

349
00:18:48.400 --> 00:18:58.400
like peeling off clothes and handing me towels to get it out of my eyes and hair and everything.
帮我脱掉衣服，递给我毛巾，好把辣椒从我的眼睛、头发和全身清理掉。

350
00:18:59.700 --> 00:19:03.820
But yes, it was, the actual spilling of it was one thing.
但没错，洒出来的那一下确实是一次拍成的。

351
00:19:03.820 --> 00:19:05.340
And did it smell?
那有味道吗？

352
00:19:05.660 --> 00:19:11.400
Because Phil Shea said they just used a gazillion cans of actual chili.
因为菲尔·谢伊说他们用了数不清罐的真辣椒酱。

353
00:19:11.800 --> 00:19:13.720
I mean, it was spicy chili.
我是说，那是辣味辣椒酱。

354
00:19:14.060 --> 00:19:18.340
Did you like put your head on the pillow that night and still smell chili?
那天晚上你把头枕在枕头上时，还能闻到辣椒味吗？

355
00:19:18.640 --> 00:19:20.780
I can tell you he smelled at dinner.
我可以告诉你，他在吃晚饭时身上有味儿。

356
00:19:21.820 --> 00:19:23.640
That's what I was about to say.
我正想说这个。

357
00:19:23.740 --> 00:19:24.100
We got to share that.
我们得分享一下那件事。

358
00:19:24.100 --> 00:19:40.920
So, Angela and Oscar and I did regular dinners where it was like, okay, let's dress up a little, like let's look like real people, take off our Dunder Mifflin clothes,
安吉拉、奥斯卡和我经常一起吃晚餐，当时我们就想，好吧，我们要稍微打扮一下，看起来像个正常人，脱掉邓德·米夫林的衣服，

359
00:19:40.960 --> 00:19:42.420
and let's go to a nice dinner.
然后去吃顿好的。

360
00:19:42.420 --> 00:19:48.380
And this particular night we had, and it was planned for months ahead, we were going to Mastro's in Beverly Hills.
那天晚上我们去的是比佛利山庄的马斯特罗餐厅，这可是提前好几个月就定好的。

361
00:19:48.580 --> 00:19:49.560
Very nice steakhouse.
非常高档的牛排馆。

362
00:19:50.260 --> 00:19:51.260
That's very fancy.
那确实很高级。

363
00:19:51.460 --> 00:19:54.220
We did not mess around with these accountant dinners.
我们对这些会计聚餐可是很认真的。

364
00:19:54.440 --> 00:19:54.720
Yeah.
没错。

365
00:19:54.960 --> 00:19:56.300
No, we did not mess around.
不，我们绝不含糊。

366
00:19:56.300 --> 00:20:01.040
And so, of course, because I was, that was the night we were filming this.
当然，因为那天晚上我们刚好在拍那场戏。

367
00:20:01.180 --> 00:20:03.020
So, everybody else got to leave early.
所以其他人都能早点走。

368
00:20:03.220 --> 00:20:06.420
They were, you know, Angela got to curl her hair or whatever.
他们，你知道的，安吉拉可以去卷卷头发什么的。

369
00:20:06.820 --> 00:20:09.860
And so, I show up.
然后，我就出现了。

370
00:20:09.880 --> 00:20:11.200
That's what Angela does with free time.
安吉拉有空时就会做这些。

371
00:20:11.220 --> 00:20:14.220
If I get to go somewhere fancy, I'm curling my damn hair.
如果我要去什么高档地方，我肯定会把我的头发卷得美美的。

372
00:20:14.660 --> 00:20:15.360
Brian knows.
布莱恩知道的。

373
00:20:15.700 --> 00:20:16.960
Let's text us for you.
让我们给你发个短信吧。

374
00:20:17.000 --> 00:20:17.360
That's the thing.
关键就在这儿。

375
00:20:17.360 --> 00:20:27.340
Like, I, so I show up and know Oscar is the one, because I think you and I are a little more, I don't want to hurt Oscar's feelings if he's listening,
我，我出现的时候，我知道奥斯卡是那个点菜的人，因为我觉得你和我稍微……我不想伤害奥斯卡的感情，如果他在听的话，

376
00:20:27.460 --> 00:20:30.380
but there's always a seafood tower.
但总会有一座海鲜塔。

377
00:20:30.800 --> 00:20:32.700
Oscar orders it every time.
奥斯卡每次都点那个。

378
00:20:32.880 --> 00:20:34.060
It is obnoxious.
太招摇了。

379
00:20:34.200 --> 00:20:35.840
It takes up the whole table.
它占了整张桌子。

380
00:20:36.640 --> 00:20:37.480
A huge lobster, like crap.
巨大的龙虾，还有乱七八糟的。

381
00:20:37.480 --> 00:20:39.200
A three-tiered tower of like seafood.
三层高的海鲜塔。

382
00:20:39.620 --> 00:20:40.220
And like oysters.
还有牡蛎。

383
00:20:40.380 --> 00:20:40.820
Oysters.
牡蛎。

384
00:20:41.600 --> 00:20:44.800
Yeah, the whole, he gets the whole, he's like, yeah, we should do the tower.
对，整座塔，他会说，没错，我们应该点那个塔。

385
00:20:44.800 --> 00:20:52.000
It comes like, and it's, it's like, like steam or smoke is coming off of it, because there's so much dry ice and fanfare.
端上来的时候，上面还冒着蒸汽或烟雾，因为用了太多的干冰，排场很大。

386
00:20:52.300 --> 00:20:53.120
Yes, yes, this is true.
是的，是的，这是真的。

387
00:20:53.500 --> 00:20:55.660
So, I sit, this is 100% true.
所以，我坐下，这百分之百是真的。

388
00:20:55.780 --> 00:21:01.940
So, I sit down, and my hands, I couldn't not smell chili.
我坐下后，我的手上，我根本无法摆脱辣椒的味道。

389
00:21:02.220 --> 00:21:04.860
So, now, here's an appetizing talk for you.
好了，现在来聊点开胃的话题吧。

390
00:21:04.920 --> 00:21:05.780
Your fingernails were orange, Brian.
你的指甲是橙色的，布莱恩。

391
00:21:05.960 --> 00:21:06.060
Orange.
橙色的。

392
00:21:06.060 --> 00:21:12.320
Because you were, you were, you had gotten it a little bit off your hands, but my memory was your hands were still tinted this weird color.
因为你，你，你虽然把手上的辣椒酱洗掉了一些，但我记得你的手还是染上了一种奇怪的颜色。

393
00:21:12.320 --> 00:21:14.900
But your fingernails, forget it.
但你的指甲，别提了。

394
00:21:15.060 --> 00:21:15.940
They were orange.
它们是橙色的。

395
00:21:16.500 --> 00:21:17.020
Done.
完了。

396
00:21:17.280 --> 00:21:17.680
Done.
彻底完了。

397
00:21:17.980 --> 00:21:19.000
So, imagine that.
所以，想象一下。

398
00:21:19.420 --> 00:21:30.520
Going into a, a crab leg with, and then you bring it to your mouth, and there's just, you're smelling really bad canned chili.
去吃蟹腿，然后你把它送到嘴边，却闻到一股非常难闻的罐装辣椒味。

399
00:21:30.800 --> 00:21:31.920
Let's just put it that way.
我们就这么说吧。

400
00:21:32.520 --> 00:21:41.780
Um, yeah, it was, it was, it was, I, it was probably, there was, there was probably some tint for, for a couple of days.
嗯，是的，它，它，它，我，它大概，大概有那么几天，手上确实留下了些颜色。

401
00:21:41.980 --> 00:21:44.840
But the smell, for sure, at least 24 hours.
但那股味道，肯定至少持续了24小时。

402
00:21:45.000 --> 00:21:47.320
Well, I have another question about the cold open.
嗯，我还有一个关于开场戏的问题。

403
00:21:47.580 --> 00:21:57.920
Is it true that the choice to try to clean up the chili with various paperwork was an improvisation in the moment, where you're grabbing stuff?
那个用各种文件去清理辣椒的决定，是当时即兴发挥的吗？就是你随手抓起东西来用？

404
00:21:57.920 --> 00:21:59.580
I mean, that's amazing.
我是说，那太绝了。

405
00:22:00.440 --> 00:22:00.840
Yes.
是的。

406
00:22:00.840 --> 00:22:02.300
I love the desperation.
我喜欢那种绝望感。

407
00:22:02.740 --> 00:22:03.300
The folder thing.
就是文件夹那段。

408
00:22:03.480 --> 00:22:03.600
The folders, yeah.
文件夹，没错。

409
00:22:03.840 --> 00:22:06.720
Once I was on the ground, I got to play a little, right?
一旦我倒在地上，我就能自由发挥了，对吧？

410
00:22:06.860 --> 00:22:09.920
And so I just sort of started trying other things.
所以我开始尝试各种别的方法。

411
00:22:10.100 --> 00:22:15.020
And look, there are a lot of fans who have fallen in love with Kevin through watching the show.
听着，有很多粉丝通过看这部剧爱上了凯文。

412
00:22:15.020 --> 00:22:22.540
And I attribute that to everyone's innate desire to, to win one.
我认为这归功于每个人内心深处都渴望能赢一次。

413
00:22:22.680 --> 00:22:23.120
Yes.
没错。

414
00:22:23.240 --> 00:22:29.220
And how difficult it is to, to find those little successes.
以及想要获得那些小小的成功是多么困难。

415
00:22:29.800 --> 00:22:38.600
Brent Forrester also said that he thinks they have about eight minutes of footage of you on the ground in the chili.
布伦特·福雷斯特也说过，他觉得你们拍了大概八分钟你躺在辣椒里的素材。

416
00:22:38.960 --> 00:22:40.180
Eight minutes.
八分钟。

417
00:22:40.460 --> 00:22:41.820
Think about that, you guys.
大家想想看。

418
00:22:41.820 --> 00:22:45.400
Eight minutes is a long time to be rolling around in some chili.
在辣椒里打滚八分钟可不是短时间。

419
00:22:45.940 --> 00:22:47.260
It was a lot.
确实拍了很久。

420
00:22:47.460 --> 00:22:54.820
I know that I tried a variety of slips, which people thought was, was funny.
我知道我尝试了各种滑倒的方式，大家觉得挺好笑的。

421
00:22:55.020 --> 00:22:57.200
I remember trying, I remember trying that.
我记得我试过，我记得我试过那个。

422
00:22:57.440 --> 00:23:10.200
But you have to remember, again, it, I remember standing by a monitor as like clothes were beginning to be taken off me.
但你要记住，再说一次，我记得当时站在监视器旁，看着他们开始帮我脱掉衣服。

423
00:23:10.840 --> 00:23:12.620
To watch it, right?
为了回看拍摄效果，对吧？

424
00:23:12.860 --> 00:23:13.280
And again.
又一次。

425
00:23:13.720 --> 00:23:14.860
Oh, yeah, because you could do that.
哦，对，因为你们可以那样做。

426
00:23:15.080 --> 00:23:20.660
You could, like, rewind the tape and watch it back and decide, did we have it or not?
你们可以倒带回看，然后决定我们是不是拍到了想要的画面。

427
00:23:20.880 --> 00:23:21.360
Correct.
没错。

428
00:23:21.600 --> 00:23:24.020
They didn't use that capability on a daily basis.
他们平时不会每天都用这个功能。

429
00:23:24.240 --> 00:23:30.820
But on a major scene where you needed to, you know, there was going to be a huge reset.
但在那种需要大动干戈、重置场景的重要戏份时就会用。

430
00:23:30.820 --> 00:23:36.320
And so I remember even, you know, watching it and me feeling good about it.
所以我记得当时看着回放，自己感觉还挺满意的。

431
00:23:36.400 --> 00:23:40.920
But me feeling good about it doesn't really matter, right?
但我觉得满意没用，对吧？

432
00:23:40.920 --> 00:23:52.180
And I remember going into the showers not knowing if, you know, because, right, like, Laverne and Debbie are great hair and makeup people.
我记得当时走进淋浴间时心里没底，因为拉弗恩和黛比是顶尖的化妆师。

433
00:23:52.360 --> 00:23:57.300
Like, they're all standing by ready to try to totally reset me to go again.
她们都站在旁边准备好要帮我彻底重置，好让我再拍一次。

434
00:23:57.300 --> 00:24:03.700
And, you know, they had obviously multiple, you know, of the same outfit, et cetera.
而且她们显然准备了多套同样的服装等等。

435
00:24:04.180 --> 00:24:10.980
So, yeah, I remember that moment about, like, please, please, I think we have it.
所以，是的，我记得当时那种心情，就是求求你们，我觉得我们已经拍好了。

436
00:24:11.240 --> 00:24:13.020
I think we're good.
我觉得可以了。

437
00:24:13.780 --> 00:24:14.720
All right, Brian.
好的，布莱恩。

438
00:24:14.960 --> 00:24:17.520
We also ask everyone this question.
我们也会问每个人这个问题。

439
00:24:18.060 --> 00:24:21.020
What did you take from the set when you left?
你离开剧组时带走了什么东西吗？

440
00:24:22.840 --> 00:24:25.360
Well, I took a few things, right?
嗯，我带走了一些东西，对吧？

441
00:24:25.360 --> 00:24:30.920
So I took the diploma, Kevin's, or certificate of completion.
我带走了那张毕业证书，或者说结业证书。

442
00:24:31.860 --> 00:24:45.800
So my first ever joke in the show, when Kevin is described by Michael Scott, he says something like he's not a great accountant, but a pretty good entertainer.
我在剧里的第一个笑点，是迈克尔·斯科特形容凯文时，他说凯文会计做得不怎么样，但是个不错的艺人。

443
00:24:45.800 --> 00:24:58.760
And they cut to a B-roll shot with me with my giant pencil, which sat on my desk then for all the years, which most people don't know is a piggy bank.
然后镜头切到我的空镜，我拿着那支巨大的铅笔，它后来在我桌上放了好多年，大多数人都不知道那其实是个存钱罐。

444
00:24:59.840 --> 00:25:01.000
It's still empty.
它现在还是空的。

445
00:25:01.200 --> 00:25:07.920
But I took that, and I took my nameplate, and I...
我带走了那个，还有我的名牌，还有……

446
00:25:07.920 --> 00:25:09.200
You had a nameplate?
你居然有名牌？

447
00:25:09.220 --> 00:25:11.920
All these years, I didn't realize you had a nameplate.
这么多年了，我都没发现你有过名牌。

448
00:25:11.920 --> 00:25:13.440
I had a nameplate.
我有过名牌。

449
00:25:13.440 --> 00:25:14.240
I didn't know that.
我不知道这个。

450
00:25:14.240 --> 00:25:15.560
I didn't have a nameplate.
我没名牌。

451
00:25:16.120 --> 00:25:18.040
Well, here's the thing that's crazy.
好吧，最疯狂的事情是这样的。

452
00:25:18.260 --> 00:25:20.260
Does it say Kevin Malone?
上面写的是凯文·马龙吗？

453
00:25:20.440 --> 00:25:22.340
And here's the crazy thing, right?
最疯狂的地方在于，对吧？

454
00:25:23.760 --> 00:25:26.500
My desk was in a clump.
我的办公桌以前是在一堆杂物里。

455
00:25:26.580 --> 00:25:28.040
You couldn't even see it.
你甚至都看不见它。

456
00:25:28.600 --> 00:25:30.760
No, it faced me.
不，它是朝向我的。

457
00:25:31.300 --> 00:25:34.020
Like, why would I need it faced?
比如说，我为什么需要它朝向我？

458
00:25:34.020 --> 00:25:35.540
To remind you your name.
为了提醒你你的名字。

459
00:25:35.760 --> 00:25:37.820
Because it's not announced...
因为它没有被公布……

460
00:25:37.820 --> 00:25:39.300
You know, usually you have a nameplate.
你知道，通常你会有个名牌。

461
00:25:39.520 --> 00:25:44.100
Like, if you have an office and someone walks in, they can see immediately what your name...
比如，如果你有间办公室，有人走进来，他们能立刻看到你的名字……

462
00:25:44.100 --> 00:25:47.620
Like, if you go into a bank, it's like, oh, this is Susie Marshall.
就像你去银行，你会看到，哦，这是苏西·马歇尔。

463
00:25:48.160 --> 00:25:49.100
Hi, Susie.
嗨，苏西。

464
00:25:49.260 --> 00:25:53.320
No, it was a nameplate that faced me for 10 years.
不，那是个朝向我放了十年的名牌。

465
00:25:54.280 --> 00:25:55.700
It makes no sense.
这根本没道理。

466
00:25:55.700 --> 00:25:57.260
Behind a piece of glass.
在玻璃后面。

467
00:25:58.020 --> 00:25:58.640
Behind a...
在……后面。

468
00:25:58.640 --> 00:26:04.380
Yes, but what I'm saying is, you would have to sit in my lap to see the nameplate.
是的，但我想说的是，你得坐在我腿上才能看到那个名牌。

469
00:26:04.400 --> 00:26:06.140
This is why we didn't know we had one.
这就是为什么我们不知道我们有这东西。

470
00:26:06.780 --> 00:26:07.820
It makes no...
这根本没……

471
00:26:07.820 --> 00:26:08.540
No, I know.
不，我知道。

472
00:26:08.740 --> 00:26:10.000
There was no thing.
根本没那回事。

473
00:26:10.400 --> 00:26:12.360
So, yeah, I took the nameplate.
所以，是的，我拿走了名牌。

474
00:26:12.520 --> 00:26:14.880
I took the pencil.
我拿走了铅笔。

475
00:26:14.960 --> 00:26:17.200
And I took the jar of M&Ms.
我还拿走了那罐M&M豆。

476
00:26:17.400 --> 00:26:17.660
Aw.
噢。

477
00:26:17.860 --> 00:26:18.260
Yes.
是的。

478
00:26:18.380 --> 00:26:18.980
I love that.
我喜欢这个。

479
00:26:19.220 --> 00:26:20.620
This has been so awesome.
这真是太棒了。

480
00:26:20.920 --> 00:26:22.880
It's been so wonderful to talk to you, Brian.
能和你交谈真是太好了，布莱恩。

481
00:26:23.140 --> 00:26:25.340
Yeah, thank you for letting us interview you.
是啊，谢谢你接受我们的采访。

482
00:26:25.340 --> 00:26:28.980
You make your living interviewing people, and now you have a book out.
你以采访他人为生，现在你出了一本书。

483
00:26:29.120 --> 00:26:30.140
Tell us about your book.
跟我们聊聊你的书吧。

484
00:26:31.320 --> 00:26:33.220
Welcome to Dunder Mifflin.
欢迎来到邓德·米福林。

485
00:26:33.960 --> 00:26:34.440
The...
那个……

486
00:26:34.440 --> 00:26:37.080
I mean, we're out in time for the holidays.
我是说，我们赶在假期前出版了。

487
00:26:37.420 --> 00:26:40.580
This is prime holiday shopping moment, everyone.
现在正是假期购物的黄金时期，各位。

488
00:26:40.760 --> 00:26:41.200
Listen up.
听好了。

489
00:26:41.620 --> 00:26:44.120
This is stocking stuffer material.
这可是绝佳的圣诞袜填塞礼物。

490
00:26:44.860 --> 00:26:46.360
Brian, I've seen the book.
布莱恩，我看过那本书了。

491
00:26:46.460 --> 00:26:48.420
You're not getting that book into a stocking.
你可没法把那本书塞进袜子里。

492
00:26:48.460 --> 00:26:49.380
Well, it's thick.
嗯，它很厚。

493
00:26:49.980 --> 00:26:51.300
That's what she said.
她也是这么说的。

494
00:26:51.300 --> 00:26:51.980
There you go.
这就对了。

495
00:26:51.980 --> 00:26:53.440
It's a thick gift.
这是一份厚礼。

496
00:26:53.780 --> 00:26:54.780
It is.
确实。

497
00:26:55.100 --> 00:26:55.760
With two Cs.
有两个C的那种厚。

498
00:26:56.800 --> 00:26:57.060
All right.
好吧。

499
00:26:57.200 --> 00:26:57.540
Okay.
好的。

500
00:26:57.880 --> 00:26:58.220
Okay.
好的。

501
00:26:58.480 --> 00:26:58.660
Okay.
好的。

502
00:26:58.700 --> 00:26:59.260
There we go.
这就对了。

503
00:26:59.640 --> 00:27:00.580
Tell us about it.
跟我们说说吧。

504
00:27:00.820 --> 00:27:02.100
Welcome to Dunder Mifflin.
欢迎来到邓德·米夫林公司。

505
00:27:02.320 --> 00:27:04.260
It is, you know...
你知道，这其实……

506
00:27:04.260 --> 00:27:04.920
Gosh.
天哪。

507
00:27:05.480 --> 00:27:17.880
Now, a couple of years, I have been working on constructing a full oral history of the office
过去几年里，我一直在致力于构建一部关于办公室的完整口述历史，

508
00:27:17.880 --> 00:27:23.940
and what happened, and specifically for me, it was about a question, right?
记录所发生的一切，对我个人而言，这源于一个问题，对吧？

509
00:27:24.100 --> 00:27:29.940
And the question was, very simply, we were a big show when we were on, right?
这个问题很简单，我们播出时是一部大热剧，对吧？

510
00:27:30.000 --> 00:27:34.700
We were the number one scripted show on NBC for most of the time that we were on the air.
在我们播出的绝大部分时间里，我们是NBC收视率最高的剧集。

511
00:27:34.700 --> 00:27:42.540
But what has happened since, as you all know, the success, the huge success of your podcast,
但正如你们所知，自从你们的播客大获成功之后，

512
00:27:43.160 --> 00:27:46.520
that the show is so much bigger now than it ever was.
这部剧现在比以往任何时候都要火得多。

513
00:27:46.940 --> 00:27:54.020
And my question was, why now, seven, eight years since we've filmed a scene,
我的问题是，为什么是现在？距离我们拍摄最后一幕已经过去七八年了，

514
00:27:54.160 --> 00:28:03.200
why is this show now the undisputed champion of viewers in the world?
为什么这部剧现在成了全球观众心目中无可争议的冠军？

515
00:28:03.200 --> 00:28:07.820
Why are more people watching this show than any other show?
为什么看这部剧的人比看其他任何剧的人都要多？

516
00:28:08.080 --> 00:28:12.680
And so, for me, it was about going back to look at what happened.
所以对我来说，关键在于回溯过去，看看究竟发生了什么。

517
00:28:12.860 --> 00:28:13.240
Why?
为什么？

518
00:28:13.320 --> 00:28:14.480
Why did this happen?
为什么会这样？

519
00:28:14.680 --> 00:28:16.760
Was it about the construction of the cast?
是因为演员阵容的构建吗？

520
00:28:16.960 --> 00:28:23.860
Was it about the aesthetic that was chosen originally by Ricky Gervais and Stephen Merchant
是因为瑞奇·热维斯和斯蒂芬·莫昌特最初选择的审美风格，

521
00:28:23.860 --> 00:28:26.480
and through Greg Daniels and Ken Quapas?
以及通过格雷格·丹尼尔斯和肯·夸帕斯的呈现吗？

522
00:28:26.480 --> 00:28:33.400
So, it's really sort of a macro version of what happened.
所以，这其实是对所发生事件的一个宏观审视。

523
00:28:33.400 --> 00:28:38.640
So, I'm super excited and proud of the book and can't wait.
我对此书感到非常兴奋和自豪，迫不及待想让大家看到。

524
00:28:38.860 --> 00:28:40.300
And is it like an overview?
这是一部概述吗？

525
00:28:40.480 --> 00:28:44.560
Is it like a collection of interviews, like from your podcast, The Oral History of the Office?
就像你的播客《办公室口述历史》那样，是一系列访谈的合集吗？

526
00:28:44.920 --> 00:28:46.680
It's a little bit of everything.
算是各种内容的结合吧。

527
00:28:46.880 --> 00:28:52.620
Yeah, it really goes back and traces my journey of me attempting to answer this question.
是的，它确实回溯并追踪了我试图回答这个问题的旅程。

528
00:28:52.620 --> 00:28:56.480
So, it goes into today as well and the legacy of the show.
所以，它也涵盖了当下以及这部剧的影响力。

529
00:28:57.300 --> 00:29:01.160
But I went back to Scranton like a real journaliSt.
我像个真正的记者一样回到了斯克兰顿。

530
00:29:01.400 --> 00:29:09.120
Went back to Scranton and met with a lot of our old friends from there to talk about how, you know,
我回到斯克兰顿，见到了许多老朋友，聊了聊，你知道，

531
00:29:09.380 --> 00:29:15.880
the show was influenced directly from them and how the show has really changed that town.
这部剧是如何直接受到他们的影响，以及这部剧又是如何真正改变了那座小镇的。

532
00:29:15.880 --> 00:29:23.500
So, it's a comprehensive look, but I think fans will really enjoy it.
所以，这是一部全面的回顾，但我相信粉丝们一定会喜欢的。

533
00:29:23.560 --> 00:29:24.480
I think that's fantastic.
我觉得这太棒了。

534
00:29:24.480 --> 00:29:25.520
I'll make sure you get a copy.
我会确保送你一本。

535
00:29:25.620 --> 00:29:26.260
Well, thank you.
好的，谢谢。

536
00:29:26.560 --> 00:29:27.640
Signed, I hope.
希望能有签名。

537
00:29:27.880 --> 00:29:28.880
Do I get a signed copy?
我能得到一本签名版吗？

538
00:29:29.020 --> 00:29:29.260
Yeah.
可以啊。

539
00:29:29.780 --> 00:29:31.080
You're not saying yes.
你还没答应呢。

540
00:29:31.660 --> 00:29:32.100
Yeah, we'll see.
好吧，到时候再说。

541
00:29:32.100 --> 00:29:32.480
Yes.
好的。

542
00:29:32.700 --> 00:29:33.040
Okay.
行。

543
00:29:34.580 --> 00:29:36.420
Brian, you have another announcement.
布莱恩，你还有另一个公告要宣布。

544
00:29:36.420 --> 00:29:43.860
Well, listen, I have a podcast, The Office Deep Dive, and next year, January 4th, coming up,
听着，我有一个播客叫《办公室深度访谈》，明年1月4日，也就是即将到来的日子，

545
00:29:44.080 --> 00:29:54.120
I'm going to be extending and transitioning to a new podcast, Off the Beat, with Brian Baumgartner,
我将扩展并转型为一个新的播客，名为《Off the Beat》，由我布莱恩·鲍姆加特纳主持，

546
00:29:54.340 --> 00:29:55.180
coming in January.
一月份上线。

547
00:29:55.420 --> 00:30:00.000
So, I love talking to all the folks on The Office, right?
我喜欢和《办公室》里的所有伙伴们聊天，对吧？

548
00:30:00.000 --> 00:30:11.060
Having lengthy discussions, but really talking about the moments in between the moments that is mostly covered by other press.
进行深入的交谈，但真正探讨的是那些其他媒体报道中很少涉及的幕后时刻。

549
00:30:11.780 --> 00:30:23.260
So, starting next year, I'm going to be talking to your other favorite stars from TV, like Modern Family, Cheers, Seinfeld, Fresh Prince of Bel-Air, whoever.
所以，从明年开始，我将采访你们喜爱的其他电视明星，比如《摩登家庭》、《干杯酒吧》、《宋飞正传》、《新鲜王子妙事多》等等。

550
00:30:24.020 --> 00:30:33.520
And we're going to be focusing on important moment in these stars' lives that happen off the beat.
我们将专注于这些明星生活中那些不为人知的关键时刻。

551
00:30:33.640 --> 00:30:45.600
I had a French director who told me, which is really applicable to The Office, by the way, you can steal this, that comedy, real comedy happens off the beat.
我曾有一位法国导演告诉我，顺便说一句，这真的非常适用于《办公室》，你们可以借鉴一下，喜剧，真正的喜剧发生在节拍之外。

552
00:30:46.100 --> 00:30:54.840
And so, for me, the moments that I want to talk to people about are the moments that happened in between the important moments,
所以对我来说，我想和大家探讨的时刻，是那些发生在重要时刻之间的瞬间，

553
00:30:55.000 --> 00:30:59.320
the things that lead to those big moments that everyone else is covering.
那些通往每个人都在报道的大场面的铺垫。

554
00:30:59.460 --> 00:31:04.520
So, I'm super excited to be expanding from The Office.
所以，我非常兴奋能从《办公室》扩展开来。

555
00:31:04.620 --> 00:31:10.100
Obviously, we'll keep checking in with The Office, but talking to people from your other favorite television shows.
显然，我们会继续关注《办公室》，但也会和你们其他最喜欢的电视节目的主创交流。

556
00:31:10.200 --> 00:31:11.740
That is fantastic, Brian.
那太棒了，布莱恩。

557
00:31:11.960 --> 00:31:12.880
We can't wait to hear it.
我们迫不及待想听听了。

558
00:31:12.900 --> 00:31:13.600
It's going to be great.
那一定会很棒。

559
00:31:13.740 --> 00:31:14.580
Thank you so much.
非常感谢。

560
00:31:14.660 --> 00:31:15.400
I appreciate it.
我很感激。

561
00:31:15.600 --> 00:31:17.820
All right, Bombers, we love you so much.
好了，Bombers们，我们非常爱你们。

562
00:31:18.020 --> 00:31:20.380
Thank you again, Brian, for this generous interview.
再次感谢你，布莱恩，接受这次慷慨的采访。

563
00:31:20.680 --> 00:31:22.140
Thanks for coming on Office, ladies.
谢谢你们来参加《办公室》节目，女士们。

564
00:31:22.500 --> 00:31:24.180
Thank you so much for having me.
非常感谢你们邀请我。

565
00:31:24.640 --> 00:31:29.580
Congratulations to everything that you guys are doing.
祝贺你们正在做的一切。

566
00:31:29.580 --> 00:31:33.140
I'm so, so happy and proud of you both.
我真的、真的为你们俩感到高兴和骄傲。

567
00:31:33.140 --> 00:31:35.560
And I love you so, so much.
我真的、真的非常爱你们。

568
00:31:35.700 --> 00:31:36.340
Love you, B.
爱你，B。

569
00:31:36.520 --> 00:31:37.460
It's going to make me cry.
这快要让我哭了。

570
00:31:37.580 --> 00:31:38.060
I know.
我知道。

571
00:31:38.400 --> 00:31:39.060
We love you, Brian.
我们爱你，布莱恩。

572
00:31:48.850 --> 00:31:51.850
Well, I loved our interview with Brian.
嗯，我很喜欢我们对布莱恩的采访。

573
00:31:52.190 --> 00:31:53.370
I loved every second.
我享受每一秒。

574
00:31:53.370 --> 00:31:55.430
It just made me miss us all hanging out.
这让我怀念我们大家聚在一起的时光。

575
00:31:55.430 --> 00:32:06.510
Now, listen, in our interview with Brian, we talked a lot about his chilly cold open, but if you can believe it, there are a few more things that we can share about it.
现在，听着，在我们对布莱恩的采访中，我们聊了很多关于他那段辣椒开场戏的内容，但如果你敢相信的话，关于它还有一些事情我们可以分享。

576
00:32:06.510 --> 00:32:15.670
So, first of all, it was written by Aaron Schur, and it was originally supposed to be the cold open for his script, Michael Scott Paper Company.
首先，它是由亚伦·舒尔编写的，最初应该是他剧本《迈克尔·斯科特造纸公司》的开场戏。

577
00:32:15.670 --> 00:32:25.890
But it ended up getting cut and moved to casual Friday, and there was a lot that our crew had to do to pull off this chilly stunt.
但它最终被删减并移到了《休闲星期五》那一集，我们的剧组为了完成这个辣椒特技做了很多工作。

578
00:32:26.370 --> 00:32:26.690
Yes.
是的。

579
00:32:26.850 --> 00:32:33.410
Our first assistant director, Rusty Mahmood, shared in an interview, and I found this so interesting, Jenna.
我们的第一助理导演拉斯蒂·马哈茂德在一次采访中分享了这一点，我觉得这太有趣了，珍娜。

580
00:32:33.410 --> 00:32:41.330
He said when he would read a script that had a cold open that was very prop intensive, it was immediately stressful for him.
他说，当他读到剧本里有需要大量道具的开场戏时，他立刻就会感到压力。

581
00:32:41.470 --> 00:32:41.870
Oh, no.
噢，不。

582
00:32:42.070 --> 00:32:48.630
So, while everyone else is laughing, he could appreciate, right, the humor, but he also saw the hard work that was going to go behind it.
所以，当其他人在大笑时，他虽然能欣赏其中的幽默，但他同时也看到了背后需要付出的艰苦努力。

583
00:32:48.710 --> 00:32:49.110
Sure.
确实。

584
00:32:49.110 --> 00:32:58.030
He said his thought process in particular for this moment was, how do we drop a huge vat of chili without destroying our office?
他说他当时思考的过程特别简单：我们怎么才能在不毁掉办公室的情况下倒下一大桶辣椒？

585
00:32:58.270 --> 00:32:58.670
Mm-hmm.
嗯哼。

586
00:32:59.050 --> 00:33:00.750
So, they had a prop meeting.
所以，他们开了一个道具会议。

587
00:33:00.750 --> 00:33:05.850
They had to discuss how big the vat was, where it was going to fall, all of that stuff that Brian talked about.
他们必须讨论桶有多大，它会掉在哪里，所有布莱恩提到的那些细节。

588
00:33:06.590 --> 00:33:06.890
Yeah.
是的。

589
00:33:07.130 --> 00:33:14.810
Randy added that on a practical note, they deliberately made this the very last scene of the week.
兰迪补充说，从实际角度出发，他们特意把这安排成那一周的最后一场戏。

590
00:33:15.070 --> 00:33:18.710
So, they could have time to clean it up before Monday, basically.
这样他们基本上就有时间在周一之前把它清理干净。

591
00:33:18.710 --> 00:33:21.170
Right, because they didn't know how bad it was going to be.
没错，因为他们不知道情况会有多糟糕。

592
00:33:21.650 --> 00:33:22.730
Oh, my gosh.
天哪。

